\begin_layout Enumerate
Apart from the dimensions named in the assignment description the sample space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 could also contain time spend studying during the course and number of lectures attended.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The label space contains the possible grades:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}\in\{-3,00,02,4,7,10,12\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
One possible loss function could be the squared error loss defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell(y,\hat{y})=(y-\hat{y})^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 which penalizes large mistakes harshly reflecting that very unfair / wrong grades should be avoided.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
One could use the following metric where 
\begin_inset Formula $x,x'\in\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 are unlabeled samples:
 
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,x')=\|x-x'\|$
\end_inset

.
 This fulfills 
\begin_inset Formula $d(a,a)=0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $d(a,b)=d(b,a)$
\end_inset

 and the triangle inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
To evaluate the performance of the hypothesis / algorithm 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 i would set aside a test set 
\begin_inset Formula $T\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $n<N$
\end_inset

 elements,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $S=\{(X_{1},Y_{1}),\dots,(X_{N},Y_{N})\}$
\end_inset

 contains all samples / data.
 I would then calculate 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{eq:empirical_risk}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 the empirical risk of the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{T}(h)=\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\ell\left(Y_{i},h(X_{i})\right),\quad(X_{i},Y_{i})\in T.\label{eq:empirical_risk}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is an unbiased estimate of the true risk 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}_{p}(h)$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $h\perp\!\!\!\perp T$
\end_inset

 holds (i.e.
 that the hypothesis is independent of the test data) and all data points in 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 are drawn from the same distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 This is quick to show:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\mathcal{R}_{T}(h)\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\ell\left(Y_{i},h(X_{i})\right)\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mathbb{E}\left[\ell\left(Y_{i},h(X_{i})\right)\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\ell\left(Y_{i},h(X_{i})\right)\mid h\right]\right]\\
 & \overset{h\perp\!\!\!\perp T}{=}\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell\left(Y,h(X)\right)\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell\left(Y,h(X)\right)\right]\\
 & =\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell\left(Y,h(X)\right)\right]=\mathcal{R}_{p}(h).
\end{align*}

\end_inset

The crucial point is that independence of the hypothesis and the training data implies that the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(X_{i},Y_{i}),$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in T$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is still just 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The students might learn about the models inner workings and start to trick it.
 For example by studying for long hours,
 but with very low intensity.
 Goodhart's law sums it up:
 
\emph on
When a measure becomes a target,
 it ceases to be a good measure.
\end_layout

