\end_inset

 measure how well the estimated hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 predicts the labels 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For the estimated model 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}=0.977$
\end_inset

.
 We see that for larger 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 the loss 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell\left(y_{i},h(x_{i})\right)=\left(y_{i}-h(x_{i})\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

 is generally smaller.
 In other words the empirical risk is smaller.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}=1$
\end_inset

 in particular:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R^{2}=1-\frac{\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(y_{i}-h(x_{i})\right)^{2}}{\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(y_{i}-\bar{y}\right)^{2}}=1\iff0=\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(y_{i}-h(x_{i})\right)^{2}\iff h(x_{i})=y_{i},\;(x_{i},y_{i})\in S.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 the hypothesis perfectly predicts the label value for all observations in 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 which for example could be the training data.
 Reversely lower values of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 indicates larger empirical risk with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R^{2}=1-\frac{\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(y_{i}-h(x_{i})\right)^{2}}{\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(y_{i}-\bar{y}\right)^{2}}=0\iff\sum^{N}_{i=1}(y_{i}-\overline{y})^{2}=\sum^{N}_{i=1}\left(y_{i}-h(x_{i})\right)^{2},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 has the same empirical risk as the hypothetical model 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{2}(x_{i})=\bar{y}$
\end_inset

 that always predicts the average output value of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be negative,
 which would indicate a hypothesis / model that performs worse than a simple average under the chosen squared difference loss function.
\end_layout

